# Migrating `BackgroundProcessor` to .NET 8

## Key Breaking Changes

1. **`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** — removed entirely in .NET 5+
2. **`ThreadAbortException` will never be raised** — catch blocks for it are dead code
3. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` also throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`**

## Why Thread.Abort Was Removed

It was inherently unsafe — it could interrupt code mid-execution (even inside `finally` blocks in some cases), corrupt state, and cause resource leaks. .NET 8 uses **cooperative cancellation** instead.

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Task? _workerTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                // Pass token so blocking calls become cancellable
                var item = await GetNextItemAsync(token);
                await ProcessItemAsync(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Graceful shutdown requested — roll back partial work
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break;
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerTask is null)
            return;

        timeout ??= TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);

        // Signal cooperative cancellation
        _cts.Cancel();

        // Wait with timeout — no forced abort needed
        if (await Task.WhenAny(_workerTask, Task.Delay(timeout.Value)) != _workerTask)
        {
            // Log warning: worker didn't stop within timeout
            // But we do NOT forcibly kill it — that's the whole point
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        _cts?.Cancel();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Migration Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---------|--------|
| **Blocking calls ignore cancellation** | If `GetNextItem()` blocks on I/O or a `Monitor.Wait`, cancellation won't interrupt it. You must pass `CancellationToken` into blocking APIs (e.g., `Channel.ReadAsync(token)`, `SemaphoreSlim.WaitAsync(token)`). |
| **`volatile bool` isn't enough** | A tight loop checking a bool won't throw; code stuck in a blocking call will never re-check. `CancellationToken` solves this via callback registration and exception propagation. |
| **Fire-and-forget `Task.Run`** | Unobserved exceptions vanish. Always `await` the task in `StopAsync` or attach a continuation. |
| **`Thread.Sleep` → `Task.Delay`** | `Thread.Sleep` blocks a thread pool thread. Use `await Task.Delay(duration, token)`. |
| **Dispose the CTS** | `CancellationTokenSource` is `IDisposable`; leaking it leaks registrations. |

## If You Can't Make Calls Async

For truly synchronous blocking code that you can't modify:

```csharp
private void ProcessLoopSync(CancellationToken token)
{
    using var reg = token.Register(() =>
    {
        // Unblock the stuck call externally, e.g.:
        // _blockingCollection.CompleteAdding();
        // _socket.Close();
    });

    while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        try
        {
            var item = GetNextItem(); // synchronous
            ProcessItem(item);
        }
        catch (InvalidOperationException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            RollbackCurrentTransaction();
            break;
        }
        finally
        {
            FlushBuffers();
        }
    }
}
```

The key insight: **you must design an escape hatch** for every blocking call. There is no safe universal "force-stop" mechanism in .NET 8 — and that's by design.
